#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

void extract_token(const char *src, char *dest, size_t dest_size) {
    size_t i = 0;
    while (src[i] != '\0' && src[i] != ',' && i < dest_size) {
        dest[i] = src[i];
        i++;
    }
    dest[i] = '\0';
}
```

The bug is in the loop condition: `i < dest_size` allows `i` to reach `dest_size - 1` and write the null terminator at index `dest_size`, which is out of bounds. The fix: change the loop condition to `i < dest_size - 1` to reserve space for the null terminator. Additionally, handle the case where the token is exactly `dest_size - 1` characters: the loop will copy that many chars, then `i` will be `dest_size - 1`, and we write null at `dest[i]` which is valid. Here's the corrected function:

```c
void extract_token(const char *src, char *dest, size_t dest_size) {
    if (dest_size == 0) return;
    size_t i = 0;
    while (src[i] != '\0' && src[i] != ',' && i < dest_size - 1) {
        dest[i] = src[i];
        i++;
    }
    dest[i] = '\0';
}
```

